# Star Trek (—not actually about Star Trek)

It was a small, church youth-group event. Barely that. A few young teens, myself barely out of pre-adolescence, getting together to watch a movie. Maybe it was a sports game, I dunno. It wasn’t fantasy or sci-fi, so it was boring and dull to me. Can I watch Star Trek: The Next Generation instead? Because I’m kind of a little shit, and have yet to develop any real self-awareness, or genuine politeness behind my dutifully-memorized manners. There’s a TV in just the next room, I can watch it there. Zachary declares that he’d rather watch that, too, and goes with me. I don’t know Zachary, he’s Jeremiah’s friend. I barely know Jeremiah, either. But it’s his house we’re at. I think.

So we watch. Jean-Luc and Geordi and Data and Dianna. Star Trek isn’t boring. Star Trek is never boring.

But maybe Zachary is bored anyway, because he starts talking about not-Star Trek. He asks what I think about gay people. Kinda out of the blue, with no provocation. I don’t think Geordi was doing anything particularly homosexual in that particular episode, but maybe I missed something.

Well, like Hermione Granger straining to raise her hand higher than high, I know this one! I’m a pastor’s kid, a Bible-fearing, Christian boy, so I cheerfully and confidently reply, “I hate ’em!”

He shrinks a little, then says “well, I’m not really gay, I just like kissing guys and stuff.”

Oh. He meant himself, I guess. He meant, do I like him. Am I okay with him. Who he is. How God made him.

Not that most of that occurred to me. I mean, I figured out pretty quick he meant he was gay, or same-sex attracted, which is close enough. But no thoughts beyond that. Because Star Trek is freaking awesome. Who needs to think about stuff when Star Trek is playing on a TV right in front of you?

We silently continue watching for a bit. He’s talking again. Would I mind if he just laid his head against my chest as we watch?

I hate gays. But not really. I don’t have any actual feelings about gay people, I just know they’re bad and that I hate them, because every good, Bible-believing Christian knows “teh gay” is bad. I mean, alright, you’re not actually s’posed to hate ’em. Hate the sin, love the sinner. Hate the gayness, love the gayboy who didn’t ask to be born gay. But if you expect me to be competent at keeping such things straight at age 13, well, I’m sorry to disappoint.

I don’t hate gays. I don’t hate Zachary. I don’t love the idea of having some dude’s head on my chest... but I don’t hate it either, or even dislike it. I don’t have any feelings about it at all. I do have very strong feelings about Star Trek. Zachary seems to have feelings that mean he would prefer to be resting his head on my chest than not.

I guess I’m okay with that. Like I said, I don’t have any feelings about it, one way or the other. The Bible says guys shouldn’t fuck guys, or “reveal their nakedness” anyway. Doesn’t say anything against resting heads on chests. Hell, the Bible does say a fair bit about John the Apostle, “the disciple whom Jesus loved”, reclining on Jesus’s bosom. I nod, and mumble something in assent. There’s Star Trek to be watched, why waste time thinking or gabbing about whose head is resting on whose body.

Poor Zach. I don’t know what his life had been like up to then, I don’t know where it went from there, what he’s doing now, or what happened to him when I shared this confusing encounter to my folks, who angrily called Jeremiah’s folks to gnaw on how that boy “made homosexual advances” toward me. That same language was used by an unconnected, adult family friend a week later, “I heard you were targeted... I’m so sorry to hear that some boy made a pass at you”. 

I said “yeah.” I didn’t know what “advances” or “made a pass” meant, but it didn’t sound exactly like what I’d gone through. Kind of sounds like someone threatening me with a knife or something. I didn’t feel threatened. But when i’d told my folks what happened, they’d called it “advances”, so I agreed. I’d been subjected to “advances”. Whatever that means.

As an adult, and one whose views on human sexuality have diverged substantially from my upbringing, this is one of those memories that pop up from time to time, one of those regrets I can’t go back to repair. Imagine being so lonely, so starved for attention and understanding, that you have to beg for touch from someone who just proudly announced that he despises you for feeling some way. For being lost in the “wrong” kind of attraction. ...And then getting in whatever trouble for it the next day. The 2020’s can still be a hard time for the gay community (particularly during the orange-skinned guy’s regime); in the 1990’s, we were just barely poking our heads up from the near-universal homophobia of the 80’s and prior. It must have been scary as fuck to come to the realization that all the tingling you feel when you’re around attractive people, the vocal timbres that drive you wild, the curvatures and outlines that dazzle you... make you one of the most hated groups in the country, if not the planet. Just for who makes your heart sing when they’re near.

Whenever I think back on this, I’m sorry for my part in making someone feel more hated, more afraid, just for wanting to be loved, and sorry for permitting others to use a harmless expression of desperate loneliness as a cause for panic and demonizing.


